1 REM ============================================================
2 REM  t56_networking.bpp
3 REM  BASIC++ Networking Test & Tutorial (FujiNet Module)
4 REM ============================================================
5 REM
6 REM  BASIC++ provides networking through the FujiNet module,
7 REM  inspired by the FujiNet hardware for Atari 8-bit computers.
8 REM  The FujiNet module provides three virtual devices:
9 REM
10 REM  N:      - Network device for HTTP, TCP, UDP connections
11 REM  FUJI:   - FujiNet control device (configuration)
12 REM  CLOCK:  - Network time protocol (NTP) clock device
13 REM
14 REM  ACTIVATING THE MODULE:
15 REM    MODULE ACTIVATE "FUJINET"
16 REM  This registers the N:, FUJI:, and CLOCK: devices
17 REM  in the virtual device system.
18 REM
19 REM  BASIC USAGE (conceptual - actual network requires hardware):
20 REM    MODULE ACTIVATE "FUJINET"
21 REM    OPEN "N:http://example.com/" FOR INPUT AS #1
22 REM    WHILE NOT EOF(1)
23 REM      INPUT #1, A$
24 REM      PRINT A$
25 REM    WEND
26 REM    CLOSE #1
27 REM
28 REM  NOTE: These tests verify the module system works without
29 REM  crashing. Actual network I/O requires a real network
30 REM  connection and server, so we test the framework only.
31 REM
32 DIALECT "GWBS"
33 REM
100 REM === MODULE ACTIVATE "FUJINET" ===
101 REM The module system allows activating/deactivating features.
102 REM FUJINET registers the N:, FUJI:, and CLOCK: virtual devices.
103 REM If the module is already registered, ACTIVATE is a no-op.
110 TEST "FujiNet module activate"
120 MODULE ACTIVATE "FUJINET"
130 REM Module activated without error
140 ENDTEST
200 REM === DEVICES listing ===
201 REM After activating FUJINET, the DEVICES command should
202 REM show N:, FUJI:, and CLOCK: in the device list.
203 REM DEVICES prints all registered virtual devices.
210 TEST "Device listing"
220 DEVICES
230 REM Should list all registered devices including FujiNet
240 ENDTEST
300 REM === N: device info ===
301 REM The N: device is the primary network device.
302 REM It supports HTTP GET/POST, TCP, and UDP protocols.
303 REM
304 REM SUPPORTED PROTOCOLS (via URL scheme):
305 REM   N:http://host/path   - HTTP GET
306 REM   N:https://host/path  - HTTPS (if supported)
307 REM   N:tcp://host:port    - Raw TCP connection
308 REM   N:udp://host:port    - UDP datagram
309 REM
310 REM Security note: Network access requires appropriate
311 REM security level (SEC_OPEN or SEC_TRUSTED).
320 TEST "N: device exists"
330 VDEV
340 REM VDEV lists all virtual devices with their capabilities.
350 REM N: should appear as a network stream device.
360 ENDTEST
400 REM === CLOCK: device concept ===
401 REM The CLOCK: device provides NTP (Network Time Protocol)
402 REM access. When opened, it connects to an NTP server
403 REM and returns the current UTC time.
404 REM
405 REM Usage:
406 REM   OPEN "CLOCK:" FOR INPUT AS #1
407 REM   INPUT #1, T$
408 REM   PRINT "UTC Time: "; T$
409 REM   CLOSE #1
420 TEST "CLOCK: device info"
430 REM CLOCK: is registered by the FujiNet module
440 REM We can't test actual NTP without network
450 ENDTEST
500 REM === FUJI: control device ===
501 REM The FUJI: device provides FujiNet configuration.
502 REM It can:
503 REM   - List available WiFi networks (SCAN)
504 REM   - Show connection status
505 REM   - Configure host slots for disk/network access
506 REM
507 REM On real FujiNet hardware, this controls the ESP32
508 REM SIO device. In BASIC++, it's a virtual equivalent.
510 TEST "FUJI: device info"
520 REM FUJI: is registered by the FujiNet module
530 REM Configuration is hardware-dependent
540 ENDTEST
600 REM === MODULE DEACTIVATE ===
601 REM You can deactivate a module to free its resources.
602 REM The virtual devices it registered are unregistered.
610 TEST "FujiNet module deactivate"
620 MODULE DEACTIVATE "FUJINET"
630 REM Module deactivated without error
640 REM Re-activate for rest of program
650 MODULE ACTIVATE "FUJINET"
660 ENDTEST
9999 END
